ule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+6(u{e}^{-6x}) & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-6u{e}^{-6x}+6u{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18284b2b-4baa-542f-b3e9-f9b101400186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308940} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff5d425-2d91-50b8-9bcc-9692165610b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308993} {:answer/id #uuid "53b8cc38-caf9-50a1-a118-c87e677f7809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} {:answer/id #uuid "68df02b9-4559-5126-84a0-ba28cb02fc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309555} {:answer/id #uuid "ac205e92-9455-5d91-bfba-6e02ad69a22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b8cc38-caf9-50a1-a118-c87e677f7809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} :answer-field/id #uuid "464e3dff-e0cc-5e85-ad7a-58ca73b13764" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554c57fc-e876-56b7-94d6-d7df7bf38bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392} {:answer/id #uuid "597f39be-9a1f-5d38-9861-17c41bcc4e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309422} {:answer/id #uuid "5e975f0c-6327-5a3e-9a4a-3ba400131efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309456} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe953db-5211-5d7f-8fac-fc01e86f8803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554c57fc-e876-56b7-94d6-d7df7bf38bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7d836f-6d18-5bf8-a30a-8cd709e799d5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149d4e7b-e129-51ce-9d5c-05e86d24d773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308902} {:answer/id #uuid "8e85b76c-5f96-59bb-a728-d1a1b2826e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309882} {:answer/id #uuid "a8782715-ec59-53ed-8453-10ff273baec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310107} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a9ec6-48f6-5fda-9b04-48714a713242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a9ec6-48f6-5fda-9b04-48714a713242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6871c91-599d-5c8a-9961-f82fb77091f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a060a8-89ee-5d43-a547-481f4dd7a9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337518" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bd1c52-2c1a-5a9b-8db0-abe912c62f09" :answer/type 